onvexity it would also contain \\((i_1,p(i_1))\\), a forbidden square.  \nHence every tile lies wholly in \\(L\\) or wholly in \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.1  The left part \\(L\\)\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) let  \n\n\\[\na_i:=p(i)-1=|\\{j:j<p(i)\\}|\n\\]\n\n(the number of squares of row \\(i\\) that belong to \\(L\\)).  \nBecause \\(p\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\(\\{a_i\\}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).\n\nSort the rows in decreasing order of \\(a_i\\); after this permutation of rows the set \\(L\\) becomes the Ferrers board  \n\n\\[\nY_L=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le n-i\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. a Young diagram whose row‑lengths are \\(n-1,n-2,\\dots ,0\\).\n\nA tiling of the original \\(L\\) by rectangles induces a tiling of \\(Y_L\\) after the same row permutation.  \nReordering rows cannot decrease the number of rectangles: a rectangle that was a product of a contiguous interval of rows remains a product of a (possibly non‑contiguous) set of rows, which can be split into at most the same number of rectangles.  \nTherefore the minimum number of rectangles needed for \\(L\\) is at least the minimum number needed for \\(Y_L\\).\n\nIn \\(Y_L\\) consider the “staircase’’ cells  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(i,n-i):1\\le i\\le n-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nTwo distinct cells of \\(S\\) cannot lie in the same axis‑parallel rectangle, because any rectangle containing \\((i,n-i)\\) and \\((i',n-i')\\) with \\(i<i'\\) would also contain the cell \\((i,n-i')\\), which belongs to the diagonal \\(\\{(k,k)\\}\\) and is absent from \\(Y_L\\).  \nHence each rectangle can contain at most one element of \\(S\\).  \nSince \\(|S|=n-1\\), any tiling of \\(Y_L\\) (and thus of \\(L\\)) requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.2  The right part \\(R\\)\n\nApply the same reasoning to \\(R\\).  \nIf we sort rows in **increasing** order of \\(a_i\\) (equivalently, de