"### Summary  \n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have solved the problem completely. The minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Permutation model.**  \n   Let \\(n=2025\\).  \n   In each row \\(i\\) the unique uncovered square is \\((i,p(i))\\) where \\(p\\) is a permutation of \\(\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).  \n   Consequently column \\(j\\) has the uncovered square \\((p^{-1}(j),j)\\).\n\n2. **Division into two monotone regions.**  \n   Define  \n   \\[\n   L=\\{(i,j):j<p(i)\\},\\qquad   \n   R=\\{(i,j):j>p(i)\\}.\n   \\]\n   No rectangle (tile) can contain a cell of \\(L\\) and a cell of \\(R\\) simultaneously; otherwise it would also contain the forbidden square \\((i,p(i))\\).  \n   Hence every tile lies completely inside \\(L\\) or completely inside \\(R\\).\n\n3. **Ferrers (Young) diagram after sorting rows.**  \n   The multiset \\(\\{p(i)-1\\}_{i=1}^{n}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \n   By permuting rows in decreasing order of \\(p(i)\\) the set \\(L\\) becomes the Ferrers board  \n\n   \\[\n   Y_L=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le n-i\\},\n   \\]\n\n   i.e. a Young diagram whose row\u2011lengths are \\(n-1,n-2,\\dots ,0\\).  \n   Re\u2011ordering rows cannot decrease the number of rectangles needed, because a rectangle that was a product of a contiguous interval of rows may split into at most the same number of rectangles after the permutation.\n\n4. **A lower bound for a Ferrers board.**  \n   In \\(Y_L\\) consider the \u201cstaircase\u2019\u2019 cells  \n\n   \\[\n   S=\\{(i,n-i):1\\le i\\le n-1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Two distinct cells of \\(S\\) cannot belong to the same axis\u2011parallel rectangle: if a rectangle contained \\((i,n-i)\\) and \\((i',n-i')\\) with \\(i<i'\\), then its column interval would be \\([\\,n-i',\\,n-i\\,]\\) and its row interval \\([\\,i,\\,i'\\,]\\); the cell \\((i,n-i')\\) lies in this rectangle but is not a member of \\(Y_L\\) (it lies on the main diagonal).  \n   Consequently each rectangle can contain **at most one** cell of \\(S\\).  \n   Since \\(|S|=n-1\\), any tiling of \\(Y_L\\) (and therefore of \\(L\\)) needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n5. **The same bound for \\(R\\).**  \n   Performing the symmetric construction (sorting rows in increasing order of \\(p(i)\\)) turns \\(R\\) into an identical Ferrers board, so covering \\(R\\) also requires at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles.\n\n6. **Total lower bound.**  \n   The families of rectangles covering \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) are disjoint, hence any admissible tiling of the whole board uses at least  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n   \\]\n\n   tiles.\n\n7. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\); the uncovered squares are the main diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\).  \n   For each column \\(j\\) (\\(2\\le j\\le n\\)) place the vertical tile  \n\n   \\[\n   T^{\\text{up}}_j=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le j-1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   covering the part of column \\(j\\) **above** the diagonal.  \n   For each column \\(j\\) (\\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\)) place the vertical tile  \n\n   \\[\n   T^{\\text{down}}_j=\\{(i,j):j+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n   \\]\n\n   covering the part of column \\(j\\) **below** the diagonal.  \n   Each \\(T^{\\text{up}}_j\\) and \\(T^{\\text{down}}_j\\) is a rectangle (a \\((j-1)\\times1\\) or \\((n-j)\\times1\\) tile) and none contains a diagonal cell.  \n   The two families are pairwise disjoint and together they cover every non\u2011diagonal unit square exactly once.  \n   The number of tiles used is  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n   \\]\n\n8. **Conclusion for \\(n=2025\\).**  \n   Substituting \\(n=2025\\) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n   \\]\n\n   The lower bound and the explicit construction coincide, so the minimum possible number of tiles is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nn=2025,\n\\qquad \np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nbe a permutation.  \nIn row \\(i\\) the unique uncovered unit square is \\((i,p(i))\\); consequently column \\(j\\) has the uncovered square \\((p^{-1}(j),j)\\).  \nAll other unit squares must be covered by axis\u2011parallel rectangular tiles, each tile being a product of a contiguous interval of rows and a contiguous interval of columns.  \nNo two tiles may overlap and no tile may contain an uncovered square.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The left and right regions  \n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nL=\\{(i,j):j<p(i)\\},\\qquad \nR=\\{(i,j):j>p(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\n*Claim.* Every tile lies completely inside \\(L\\) or completely inside \\(R\\).\n\n*Proof.* Let a tile be the rectangle  \n\n\\[\nT=[r_1,r_2]\\times[c_1,c_2],\\qquad r_1\\le r_2,\\;c_1\\le c_2 .\n\\]\n\nIf there exist rows \\(i\\in[r_1,r_2]\\) with \\(p(i)<c_1\\) (a point of \\(L\\)) and a row \\(i'\\in[r_1,r_2]\\) with \\(p(i')>c_2\\) (a point of \\(R\\)), then because the values \\(\\{p(i)\\mid i\\in[r_1,r_2]\\}\\) are distinct, one of them must lie in the interval \\([c_1,c_2]\\); that would be the uncovered square \\((i'',p(i''))\\) belonging to \\(T\\), contradicting the hypothesis.  \nHence either \\(p(i)\\le c_1-1\\) for all \\(i\\in[r_1,r_2]\\) (so \\(T\\subseteq L\\)) or \\(p(i)\\ge c_2+1\\) for all such \\(i\\) (so \\(T\\subseteq R\\)). \u220e\n\nThus the problem splits into two independent covering problems: covering \\(L\\) and covering \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lower bound for covering \\(L\\)\n\nFor each row \\(i\\) put  \n\n\\[\na_i:=p(i)-1=|\\{j:j<p(i)\\}|.\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(p\\) is a permutation, the multiset \\(\\{a_i\\}\\) equals \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nReorder the rows so that  \n\n\\[\na_{1}\\ge a_{2}\\ge\\cdots\\ge a_{n}.\n\\]\n\nAfter this permutation the set \\(L\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\nY_L=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le a_i\\}\n      =\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le n,\\;1\\le j\\le n-i\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. a Ferrers (Young) diagram whose row\u2011lengths are \\(n-1,n-2,\\dots ,0\\).\n\nA rectangle that covered some cells of the original \\(L\\) is a product of a **contiguous** interval of original rows and a contiguous interval of columns.  \nAfter the row permutation the same rectangle may become a product of a **non\u2011contiguous** set of rows; it can be split into at most the same number of rectangles whose row sets are contiguous.  \nConsequently the minimum number of rectangles needed for the original \\(L\\) is at least the minimum number needed for \\(Y_L\\).\n\nNow consider the \u201cstaircase\u2019\u2019 cells  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(i,n-i):1\\le i\\le n-1\\}\\subseteq Y_L .\n\\]\n\n*Lemma.* No two distinct cells of \\(S\\) can belong to the same axis\u2011parallel rectangle contained in \\(Y_L\\).\n\n*Proof.* Suppose a rectangle \\(R=[r_1,r_2]\\times[c_1,c_2]\\subseteq Y_L\\) contains \\((i,n-i)\\) and \\((i',n-i')\\) with \\(i<i'\\).  \nBecause the rows increase, we have \\(r_1\\le i<i'\\le r_2\\); because the column indices satisfy \\(n-i>n-i'\\), we have \\(c_1\\le n-i'\\) and \\(c_2\\ge n-i\\).  \nHence the cell \\((i,n-i')\\) lies in \\(R\\).  \nBut \\((i,n-i')\\) is on the main diagonal (\\(i=n-i'\\)), which is **not** a member of \\(Y_L\\) (the Ferrers diagram contains only cells strictly left of the diagonal).  \nThus \\(R\\) cannot be wholly contained in \\(Y_L\\), a contradiction. \u220e\n\nSince each rectangle can contain at most one element of \\(S\\) and \\(|S|=n-1\\), at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover \\(Y_L\\), and therefore at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are required to cover the original set \\(L\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound for covering \\(R\\)\n\nApply the symmetric argument to the set  \n\n\\[\nR=\\{(i,j):j>p(i)\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf we reorder the rows in **increasing** order of \\(p(i)\\) (equivalently decreasing order of \\(b_i:=n-p(i)\\)), the set \\(R\\) becomes the same Ferrers diagram \\(Y_R\\) with row\u2011lengths \\(n-1,n-2,\\dots ,0\\).  \nExactly the same reasoning as in Sections\u202f2\u20132 shows that at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles are needed to cover \\(R\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Total lower bound  \n\nSince the families of rectangles covering \\(L\\) and \\(R\\) are disjoint, any admissible tiling of the whole board uses at least  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\n\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Construction attaining the bound  \n\nChoose the identity permutation \\(p(i)=i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are the diagonal cells \\((i,i)\\), \\(1\\le i\\le n\\).\n\nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(2\\le j\\le n\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{up}}_j=\\{(i,j):1\\le i\\le j-1\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich covers the part of column \\(j\\) **above** the diagonal.  \nFor each column \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le n-1\\) place the vertical tile  \n\n\\[\nT^{\\text{down}}_j=\\{(i,j):j+1\\le i\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich covers the part of column \\(j\\) **below** the diagonal.\n\nEach \\(T^{\\text{up}}_j\\) is a \\((j-1)\\times1\\) rectangle, each \\(T^{\\text{down}}_j\\) is a \\((n-j)\\times1\\) rectangle; none contains a diagonal cell because the diagonal cell of column \\(j\\) is \\((j,j)\\), which is excluded from both definitions.  \n\nThe two families are pairwise disjoint, and together they cover every non\u2011diagonal unit square exactly once.  \nThe number of tiles used is  \n\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{tiles }T^{\\text{up}}_j}+ \n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{tiles }T^{\\text{down}}_j}=2n-2 .\n\\]\n\nThus a tiling with \\(2n-2\\) tiles exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 6.  Conclusion for the original problem  \n\nFor \\(n=2025\\),\n\n\\[\n2n-2 = 2\\cdot2025-2 = 4050-2 = 4048 .\n\\]\n\nThe lower bound \\(2n-2\\) and the explicit construction both give the same number, so the minimum possible number of tiles is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}.\n\\]"
