e row satisfies \\(p(i)=n\\).  \nConsequently the collection  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\{L_i\\mid p(i)>1\\}\\cup\\{R_i\\mid p(i)<n\\}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\ncontains precisely \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) distinct unit squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lemma: a rectangle can contain at most one element of \\(S\\)\n\nLet a rectangle be given by a row interval \\([a,b]\\) and a column interval \\([c,d]\\) (with \\(a\\le b,\\;c\\le d\\)).  \nThe rectangle is *admissible* iff it contains no uncovered square, i.e.\n\n\\[\np(r)\\notin[c,d]\\quad\\text{for every }r\\in[a,b].\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nWe prove three statements.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 2.1 (no two left‑neighbour squares).**  \nAssume the rectangle contains two distinct squares \\(L_i\\) and \\(L_j\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \nThen \\(p(i)-1,c\\le p(i)-1\\le d\\) and \\(p(j)-1\\in[c,d]\\).  \nSince the column interval is contiguous, \\(d\\ge\\max\\{p(i)-1,p(j)-1\\}\\).  \nCondition (5) applied to row \\(i\\) forces \\(p(i)>d\\); applied to row \\(j\\) forces \\(p(j)>d\\).  \nThus \\(p(i)>d\\ge p(j)-1\\) and \\(p(j)>d\\ge p(i)-1\\).  \nHence \\(p(i)\\ge p(j)\\) and \\(p(j)\\ge p(i)\\), so \\(p(i)=p(j)\\), contradicting the bijectivity of \\(p\\).  \nTherefore an admissible rectangle cannot contain two different \\(L\\)-squares.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 2.2 (no two right‑neighbour squares).**  \nThe argument of Lemma 2.1 applied to the reversed permutation \\(i\\mapsto n+1-p(i)\\) yields the same conclusion for the set \\(\\{R_i\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Lemma 2.3 (no left‑ and right‑neighbour together).**  \nSuppose the rectangle contains \\(L_i=(i,p(i)-1)\\) and \\(R_j=(j,p(j)+1)\\).  \nLet \\(c_1=p(i)-1\\) and \\(c_2=p(j)+1\\).  \nWithout loss of generality assume \\(c_1\\le c_2\\); then the column interval is \\([c_1,c_2]\\).  \nSince \\(c_2\\ge p(j)+1\\), the hole \\((j,p(j))\\) lies at column \\(p(j)=c_2-1\\), which belongs to \\([c_1,c_2]\\).  \nThus condition (5) is violated for row \\(j\\).  \nIf instead \\(c_2<c_1\\) the symmetric argument shows that the hole \\((i,p(i))\\) lies inside the rectangle.  \nHence an admissible rectangle cannot contain simultaneously an \\(L\\)-square and an \\(R\\)-square.\n\n---\n\nFrom Lemmas 2.1–2.3 we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Every admissible rectangle contains at most one element of }S.}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound on the number of tiles\n\nSince the tiles form a partition of all covered squares, each tile is an admissible rectangle.  \nBy (6) each tile can cover at most one square of the set \\(S\\), which has cardinality \\(|S|=2n-2\\).  \nTherefore any tiling uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Number of tiles}\\;\\ge\\;2n-2.}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the permutation to be the identity, i.e. \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Horizontal tiles.*  \nFor each row \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the rectangle\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\le j\\le n\\}\\setminus\\{(i,i)\\}\n   =\\{(i,i+1),(i,i+2),\\dots ,(i,n)\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\\(H_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) tile lying entirely to the right of the hole in its row.\n\n*Vertical tiles.*  \nFor each column \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\) place the rectangle\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\le i\\le n\\}\\setminus\\{(j,j)\\}\n   =\\{(j+1,j),(j+2,j),\\dots ,(n,j)\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\\(V_j\\) is an \\((n-j)\\times1\\) tile lying entirely below the hole in its column.\n\nThe families \\(\\{H_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{V_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}\\) are pairwise disjoint, avoid all diagonal holes, and together cover every off‑diagonal square.  \nThe total number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus the bound (7) is tight.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5