"### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **Model the uncovered squares.**  \n   The condition \u201ceach row and each column has exactly one uncovered unit square\u2019\u2019 forces the uncovered squares to form a permutation matrix: there is a bijection \\(p:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) such that the uncovered square in row \\(i\\) is \\((i,p(i))\\).  \n   Here \\(n=2025\\).\n\n2. **Define forced squares.**  \n   For every row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)>1\\) let  \n   \\[\n   L_i=(i,p(i)-1)\n   \\]\n   (the square immediately to the left of the uncovered one).  \n   For every row \\(i\\) with \\(p(i)<n\\) let  \n   \\[\n   R_i=(i,p(i)+1)\n   \\]\n   (the square immediately to the right of the uncovered one).  \n   Exactly one row has \\(p(i)=1\\) and one row has \\(p(i)=n\\); consequently the set  \n   \\[\n   S=\\{L_i\\mid p(i)>1\\}\\cup\\{R_i\\mid p(i)<n\\}\n   \\]\n   contains \\(2n-2\\) distinct squares.\n\n3. **Lower\u2011bound lemma.**  \n   *Lemma.* No axis\u2011parallel rectangle that avoids all uncovered squares can contain two distinct squares of \\(S\\).  \n   - A rectangle cannot contain two different \\(L\\)-squares because that would force two rows to have the same hole column, contradicting the bijectivity of \\(p\\).  \n   - By symmetry a rectangle cannot contain two different \\(R\\)-squares.  \n   - A rectangle cannot contain an \\(L\\)-square and an \\(R\\)-square simultaneously: if it contained \\((i,p(i)-1)\\) and \\((j,p(j)+1)\\), its column interval would contain both \\(p(i)-1\\) and \\(p(j)+1\\); whichever of the two endpoints is larger would also contain the corresponding hole \\((i,p(i))\\) or \\((j,p(j))\\), which is forbidden.  \n\n   Hence each tile covers at most one element of \\(S\\). Since \\(|S|=2n-2\\), any tiling needs at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles.\n\n4. **Construction attaining the bound.**  \n   Choose the uncovered squares to be the main diagonal, i.e. \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \n   - For each row \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place a horizontal tile covering the squares \\((i,i+1),(i,i+2),\\dots ,(i,n)\\).  \n   - For each column \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\) place a vertical tile covering the squares \\((j+1,j),(j+2,j),\\dots ,(n,j)\\).  \n\n   These \\( (n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) tiles are pairwise disjoint, avoid the diagonal holes, and together cover every other unit square.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   The lower bound shows that no placement can use fewer than \\(2n-2\\) tiles, and the explicit construction shows that \\(2n-2\\) tiles are sufficient. Therefore the minimum possible number of tiles is exactly \\(2n-2\\).  \n   For \\(n=2025\\) this equals \\(2\\cdot2025-2=4048\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4048}\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet \\(n=2025\\).  \nA placement of tiles satisfying \u201ceach row and each column has exactly one uncovered unit square\u2019\u2019 determines a permutation\n\\[\np:\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\longrightarrow\\{1,\\dots ,n\\},\n\\qquad\\text{with uncovered square }(i,p(i))\\text{ in row }i .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nAll other squares must be covered by axis\u2011parallel rectangles (tiles) whose interiors contain no point of the set\n\\(\\{(i,p(i))\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Forced neighbour squares\n\nFor a row \\(i\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n(i,p(i)-1), &\\text{if }p(i)>1,\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{undefined}, &\\text{if }p(i)=1 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n\\(L_i\\) is the square immediately to the **left** of the uncovered square in row \\(i\\).  \nSimilarly define  \n\n\\[\nR_i=\n\\begin{cases}\n(i,p(i)+1), &\\text{if }p(i)<n,\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{undefined}, &\\text{if }p(i)=n .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n\\(R_i\\) is the square immediately to the **right** of the uncovered square in row \\(i\\).\n\nBecause \\(p\\) is a bijection, exactly one row satisfies \\(p(i)=1\\) and exactly one row satisfies \\(p(i)=n\\).  \nConsequently the collection  \n\n\\[\nS:=\\{L_i\\mid p(i)>1\\}\\cup\\{R_i\\mid p(i)<n\\}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\ncontains precisely \\((n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\\) distinct unit squares.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Lemma: a rectangle can contain at most one element of \\(S\\)\n\nLet a rectangle be given by a row interval \\([a,b]\\) and a column interval \\([c,d]\\) (with \\(a\\le b,\\;c\\le d\\)).  \nThe rectangle is *admissible* iff it contains no uncovered square, i.e.\n\n\\[\np(r)\\notin[c,d]\\quad\\text{for every }r\\in[a,b].\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nWe prove three statements.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma\u202f2.1 (no two left\u2011neighbour squares).**  \nAssume the rectangle contains two distinct squares \\(L_i\\) and \\(L_j\\) with \\(i<j\\).  \nThen \\(p(i)-1,c\\le p(i)-1\\le d\\) and \\(p(j)-1\\in[c,d]\\).  \nSince the column interval is contiguous, \\(d\\ge\\max\\{p(i)-1,p(j)-1\\}\\).  \nCondition (5) applied to row \\(i\\) forces \\(p(i)>d\\); applied to row \\(j\\) forces \\(p(j)>d\\).  \nThus \\(p(i)>d\\ge p(j)-1\\) and \\(p(j)>d\\ge p(i)-1\\).  \nHence \\(p(i)\\ge p(j)\\) and \\(p(j)\\ge p(i)\\), so \\(p(i)=p(j)\\), contradicting the bijectivity of \\(p\\).  \nTherefore an admissible rectangle cannot contain two different \\(L\\)-squares.\n\n---\n\n**Lemma\u202f2.2 (no two right\u2011neighbour squares).**  \nThe argument of Lemma\u202f2.1 applied to the reversed permutation \\(i\\mapsto n+1-p(i)\\) yields the same conclusion for the set \\(\\{R_i\\}\\).\n\n---\n\n**Lemma\u202f2.3 (no left\u2011 and right\u2011neighbour together).**  \nSuppose the rectangle contains \\(L_i=(i,p(i)-1)\\) and \\(R_j=(j,p(j)+1)\\).  \nLet \\(c_1=p(i)-1\\) and \\(c_2=p(j)+1\\).  \nWithout loss of generality assume \\(c_1\\le c_2\\); then the column interval is \\([c_1,c_2]\\).  \nSince \\(c_2\\ge p(j)+1\\), the hole \\((j,p(j))\\) lies at column \\(p(j)=c_2-1\\), which belongs to \\([c_1,c_2]\\).  \nThus condition (5) is violated for row \\(j\\).  \nIf instead \\(c_2<c_1\\) the symmetric argument shows that the hole \\((i,p(i))\\) lies inside the rectangle.  \nHence an admissible rectangle cannot contain simultaneously an \\(L\\)-square and an \\(R\\)-square.\n\n---\n\nFrom Lemmas\u202f2.1\u20132.3 we obtain:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Every admissible rectangle contains at most one element of }S.}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Lower bound on the number of tiles\n\nSince the tiles form a partition of all covered squares, each tile is an admissible rectangle.  \nBy (6) each tile can cover at most one square of the set \\(S\\), which has cardinality \\(|S|=2n-2\\).  \nTherefore any tiling uses at least \\(2n-2\\) tiles:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Number of tiles}\\;\\ge\\;2n-2.}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction attaining the bound\n\nChoose the permutation to be the identity, i.e. \\(p(i)=i\\) for all \\(i\\).  \nThe uncovered squares are precisely the diagonal squares \\((i,i)\\).\n\n*Horizontal tiles.*  \nFor each row \\(i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\) place the rectangle\n\\[\nH_i=\\{(i,j)\\mid i\\le j\\le n\\}\\setminus\\{(i,i)\\}\n   =\\{(i,i+1),(i,i+2),\\dots ,(i,n)\\}.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\\(H_i\\) is a \\(1\\times (n-i)\\) tile lying entirely to the right of the hole in its row.\n\n*Vertical tiles.*  \nFor each column \\(j=2,\\dots ,n\\) place the rectangle\n\\[\nV_j=\\{(i,j)\\mid j\\le i\\le n\\}\\setminus\\{(j,j)\\}\n   =\\{(j+1,j),(j+2,j),\\dots ,(n,j)\\}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\\(V_j\\) is an \\((n-j)\\times1\\) tile lying entirely below the hole in its column.\n\nThe families \\(\\{H_i\\}_{i=1}^{n-1}\\) and \\(\\{V_j\\}_{j=2}^{n}\\) are pairwise disjoint, avoid all diagonal holes, and together cover every off\u2011diagonal square.  \nThe total number of tiles is\n\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2.\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThus the bound (7) is tight.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion for \\(n=2025\\)\n\nSubstituting \\(n=2025\\) into \\(2n-2\\) yields\n\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2=4048 .\n\\]\n\nHence the minimum possible number of rectangular tiles is \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n\n---\n\n### Self-Correction Instruction ###\n\nPlease review the **Summary** and **Detailed Solution** sections to ensure:\n* All statements are fully justified and no step is omitted.\n* Every mathematical expression is enclosed in TeX delimiters.\n* The final answer is presented exactly as \\(\\boxed{4048}\\).\n* The logical flow follows the outline given in the summary."
