ares.  \nThen either \\(R\\subseteq A\\) or \\(R\\subseteq B\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nWrite \\(R=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\) with integer endpoints, where \\([a,b]\\) is a set of consecutive rows and \\([c,d]\\) a set of consecutive columns.  \nIf there existed a row \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) with \\(c\\le p(i)\\le d\\), then the cell \\((i,p(i))\\) would belong to \\(R\\), contradicting the hypothesis that \\(R\\) contains only covered squares.  \nHence for every \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) either \\(p(i)<c\\) (the whole rectangle lies to the right of the missing cell in that row) or \\(p(i)>d\\) (the whole rectangle lies to the left).  \nIf both possibilities occurred for different rows in the same interval \\([a,b]\\), the set \\(\\{p(i)\\mid i\\in[a,b]\\}\\) would contain a value both \\(<c\\) and \\(>d\\); but then the interval \\([c,d]\\) would intersect that set, again impossible.  \nThus either \\(p(i)<c\\) for all \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) (which means \\(R\\subseteq A\\)) or \\(p(i)>d\\) for all \\(i\\in[a,b]\\) (which means \\(R\\subseteq B\\)). \u220e\n\nConsequently any tiling of the covered squares consists of a tiling of \\(A\\) plus a tiling of \\(B\\).  \nWe treat \\(A\\); the same argument applies to \\(B\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (A needs at least \\(n-1\\) rectangles).  \n\nFor each \\(k\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) let \\(r_k\\) be the unique row with \\(p(r_k)=k\\).  \nThe cell \\((r_k,k+1)\\) belongs to \\(A\\) because \\(k+1>p(r_k)=k\\).\n\n**Claim.** No rectangle contained in \\(A\\) can contain two distinct cells \\((r_k,k+1)\\) and \\((r_{k'},k'+1)\\) with \\(k<k'\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nAssume a rectangle \\(R=[a,b]\\times[c,d]\\subseteq A\\) contains both cells.  \nThen \\(c\\le k+1\\) and \\(d\\ge k'+1\\).  \nSince \\(R\\subseteq A\\), Lemma\u202f1 forces \\(c> \\max\\{p(r_k),p(r_{k'})\\}=k'\\).  \nThus \\(c>k'\\ge k+1\\), contradicting \\(c\\le k+1\\). \u220e\n\nThe \\(n-1\\) cells \\(\\{(r_k,k+1)\\mid k=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\